WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.547 --> 00:00:06.488 line:-2 align:center
We introduce Proxy Policy Steering an inference time adaptation method for robotics foundation models

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.334 line:-2 align:center
Generalist policies carry broad manipulation priors from large scale data

3
00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:18.249 line:-2 align:center
However they still struggle in downstream tasks that are underrepresented in the training distribution

4
00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:21.300 line:-2 align:center
Direct fine tuning methods tackle this by modifying the base policy

5
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.478 line:-2 align:center
weights reshaping the base policy action distribution

6
00:00:25.107 --> 00:00:27.200 line:-2 align:center
While this effectively adapts the policy behavior

7
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.495 line:-2 align:center
it can overfit to the limited demonstrations and lose the general priors

8
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.520 line:-2 align:center
Existing steering methods like DSRL change the initial noise or

9
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.168 line:-2 align:center
flow matching process to select actions suitable for the task

10
00:00:40.358 --> 00:00:46.417 line:-2 align:center
However the sampled actions still stay within the base distribution and fail to deliver novel behaviors

11
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.151 line:-2 align:center
How could generalist policies learn new skills without losing the base prior

12
00:00:52.332 --> 00:00:54.000 line:-2 align:center
We propose Proxy Policy Steering

13
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.160 line:-2 align:center
Instead of reshaping the base action distribution we estimate how task supervision would change it

14
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:04.894 line:-2 align:center
Specifically we distill the base policy into a lightweight reference proxy under task observations

15
00:01:04.954 --> 00:01:09.306 line:-2 align:center
We then fine tune this proxy under task supervision to obtain a task proxy

16
00:01:09.644 --> 00:01:12.480 line:-2 align:center
At inference we take the velocity predictions of all three models

17
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.893 line:-2 align:center
subtract the reference velocity and add the task velocity

18
00:01:18.038 --> 00:01:20.120 line:-2 align:center
This residual captures the task induced shift

19
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.239 line:-2 align:center
steering the policy to approximate the fine tuned action distribution

20
00:01:25.692 --> 00:01:29.354 line:-3.3 align:center
Facing a state within task distribution while the base policy fails to locate the

21
00:01:29.414 --> 00:01:34.374 line:-3.3 align:center
correct grasping position the residual steers the base toward the task proxy

22
00:01:35.004 --> 00:01:39.180 line:-3.3 align:center
which guides the robot to show novel task behavior for correct grasping

23
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.395 line:-3.3 align:center
At an out of distribution state the base policy shows generic recovery behavior

24
00:01:47.455 --> 00:01:52.538 line:-3.3 align:center
Here our proxy policies share the same overfitting errors making the residual near zero

25
00:01:52.598 --> 00:01:56.892 line:-3.3 align:center
Therefore the base retains control and recovers from the grasping failure

26
00:02:01.127 --> 00:02:03.560 line:-3.3 align:center
When the state is within both the pre training distribution and

27
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:07.208 line:-3.3 align:center
the task demonstrations the three policies reach an agreement

28
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.740 line:-2 align:center
By combining proxy residual with the base prior PPS injects task guidance while retaining

29
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.859 line:-2 align:center
the general priors of the base model achieving robust manipulation in downstream tasks

30
00:02:20.712 --> 00:02:23.980 line:-2 align:center
We evaluate PPS on 12 tasks spanning long horizon goals

31
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.358 line:-2 align:center
deformable and articulated objects and diverse skills

32
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.932 line:-2 align:center
PPS outperforms LoRA fine tuning previous steering methods and trained from scratch policies

33
00:02:33.729 --> 00:02:38.249 line:-2 align:center
Since PPS keeps the base model intact it preserves the general priors from the base model

34
00:02:38.309 --> 00:02:40.900 line:-2 align:center
This helps the policy recover under human interventions even though

35
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.195 line:-2 align:center
no recovery behavior is collected in the demonstrations

36
00:02:46.998 --> 00:02:51.000 line:-2 align:center
Whereas LoRA fine tuning can overfit to the small scale task specific demonstrations and overwrite parts

37
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:56.140 line:-2 align:center
of the manipulation priors learned during pre training causing it to fail under perturbations

38
00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.500 line:-2 align:center
PPS also enables injecting information from additional modalities into the base model

39
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.913 line:-2 align:center
such as using point cloud to locate textureless objects and leveraging audio inputs to find the sound source

40
00:03:09.763 --> 00:03:10.880 line:-2 align:center
Thanks for watching
